/T (Three unexpected results demonstrated: \(1\) \(4.2-3.5\)/0.1 is not exactly 7; \(2\) int\(\(3.1-3.5\)/0.1\) gives -3 instead of -4 because int\(\) truncates toward zero; \(3\) round\(2.5\) = 2 and round\(-2.5\) = -2 \(ties go to the even number\), and also -7 // 2 = -4 and -2 ** 2 = -4. Conclusion: round\(\) is the correct choice for step counts. floor\(\) is correct here only by luck \(a value like 6.999999 would give 6\); int\(\) is wrong for negative values. Time: all three are O\(1\) per call \(round 72 ns, int 58 ns, floor 38 ns in my run\) and use O\(1\) space.)
/E (Three unexpected results demonstrated: \(1\) \(4.2-3.5\)/0.1 is not exactly 7; \(2\) int\(\(3.1-3.5\)/0.1\) gives -3 instead of -4 because int\(\) truncates toward zero; \(3\) round\(2.5\) = 2 and round\(-2.5\) = -2 \(ties go to the even number\), and also -7 // 2 = -4 and -2 ** 2 = -4. Conclusion: round\(\) is the correct choice for step counts. floor\(\) is correct here only by luck \(a value like 6.999999 would give 6\); int\(\) is wrong for negative values. Time: all three are O\(1\) per call \(round 72 ns, int 58 ns, floor 38 ns in my run\) and use O\(1\) space.)
/Pg 14 0 R
