1055 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 101 0 R
/T (Expected = the exact decimal total; actual = the float total. They differ in the 10th-14th significant digits because each float addition rounds to the nearest binary value, and these tiny errors accumulate over more additions \(differences of about 1e-10 for 300, 1e-8 for 6,000, and 5e-7 for 150,000 payments\). Decimal is exact but about 1.1-1.9 times slower; even for 150,000 payments it takes only about 31 ms, so the speed cost is not a problem.)
/E (Expected = the exact decimal total; actual = the float total. They differ in the 10th-14th significant digits because each float addition rounds to the nearest binary value, and these tiny errors accumulate over more additions \(differences of about 1e-10 for 300, 1e-8 for 6,000, and 5e-7 for 150,000 payments\). Decimal is exact but about 1.1-1.9 times slower; even for 150,000 payments it takes only about 31 ms, so the speed cost is not a problem.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
