1059 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 101 0 R
/T (How it works: compute the rate once for every possible \(fat, snf\) pair and store it in a dictionary keyed by the pair; then each entry just looks the rate up: rate = table[\(fat, snf\)]. With 0.1% resolution there are 51 fat values \(2.0-7.0\) x 31 SNF values \(7.0-10.0\) = k = 1,581 pairs. Time per entry: recalculation about 493 ns; dictionary lookup about 91 ns \(O\(1\) average\) - roughly 5 times faster. Building the table costs about 1.0 ms once \(O\(k\)\); it pays off after roughly 2,500 entries \(1.0 ms divided by the ~0.4 microsecond saved per entry\). Extra space: O\(k\) - the dictionary structure alone was 73,808 bytes for k = 1,581 \(plus the key and value objects\). Limitation: it only works when fat/SNF come from a small finite set;)
/E (How it works: compute the rate once for every possible \(fat, snf\) pair and store it in a dictionary keyed by the pair; then each entry just looks the rate up: rate = table[\(fat, snf\)]. With 0.1% resolution there are 51 fat values \(2.0-7.0\) x 31 SNF values \(7.0-10.0\) = k = 1,581 pairs. Time per entry: recalculation about 493 ns; dictionary lookup about 91 ns \(O\(1\) average\) - roughly 5 times faster. Building the table costs about 1.0 ms once \(O\(k\)\); it pays off after roughly 2,500 entries \(1.0 ms divided by the ~0.4 microsecond saved per entry\). Extra space: O\(k\) - the dictionary structure alone was 73,808 bytes for k = 1,581 \(plus the key and value objects\). Limitation: it only works when fat/SNF come from a small finite set;)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [32]
>>
endobj
