/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 103 0 R
/T (How it works: the rates are kept in a file such as {'base': '30.00', 'fat_step': '0.40', 'snf_step': '0.30'}; at start-up the program reads the file with json.load\(\) into a dictionary and converts the strings to Decimal. Start-up time: O\(t\) for t rate values - measured 1.8 microseconds for 3 values, 0.2 ms for 1,000 and about 30 ms for 100,000. Space: O\(t\) \(the 100,000-value file was about 1.8 MB\). Maintenance: rates can be revised by editing the file with no change to Python code and no redistribution of the program after each rate revision; the cost is that the file can be missing or malformed, so the program must validate it when loading \(store numbers as strings or use parse_float=Decimal so no float errors are introduced\). For the few rate values here the load cost is negligible, so file-based rates are a good improvement for maintainability.)
/E (How it works: the rates are kept in a file such as {'base': '30.00', 'fat_step': '0.40', 'snf_step': '0.30'}; at start-up the program reads the file with json.load\(\) into a dictionary and converts the strings to Decimal. Start-up time: O\(t\) for t rate values - measured 1.8 microseconds for 3 values, 0.2 ms for 1,000 and about 30 ms for 100,000. Space: O\(t\) \(the 100,000-value file was about 1.8 MB\). Maintenance: rates can be revised by editing the file with no change to Python code and no redistribution of the program after each rate revision; the cost is that the file can be missing or malformed, so the program must validate it when loading \(store numbers as strings or use parse_float=Decimal so no float errors are introduced\). For the few rate values here the load cost is negligible, so file-based rates are a good improvement for maintainability.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [18]
>>
