1468 0 obj
<<
/Title (Separate variables: 300 farmers need 900 hand-written names \(litres1...litres300, fat1..., snf1...\) and the calculation code has to be repeated or generated for each name, because a loop cannot step through variable names. Adding a farmer means editing the source code. Source-code size grows as O\(n\). List/tuple of records: one name holds all entries; one loop processes any n; source code size is O\(1\) and data is added at run time.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1343 0 R
/Next 1469 0 R
>>
endobj
1469 0 obj
<<
/Title (Complexity for n entries: processing time is O\(n\) for both \(each entry must be computed once\); memory for the data is O\(n\) for both. The difference is in code size/maintainability \(O\(n\) lines vs O\(1\)\) and in the fact that the list can be looped, sorted, filtered and validated with the same code. The list/tuple approach is far more scalable: for 6,000 or 150,000 farmers, separate variables would need 18,000 or 450,000 names, which is impractical.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1343 0 R
/Prev 1468 0 R
>>
endobj
