1699 0 obj
<<
/Title (\(a\) litres >= 0 \(zero allowed\). \(b\) 2.0 <= fat <= 7.0. \(c\) 7.0 <= snf <= 10.0 \(assumed range\). \(d\) farmer ID not empty. \(e\) shift must be 'morning' or 'evening'. \(f\) litres, fat and SNF must be valid numbers \(not text such as 'eight' or '8,5', and not nan/inf\).)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1565 0 R
>>
endobj
1700 0 obj
<<
/Title (1. Types: 10 is int, 10.0 is float, '10' is str \(text in quotes\), True is bool, [10, 8.5] is list. 2. Operators: 7 / 2 = 3.5 \(true division always gives float\); 7 // 2 = 3 \(floor division\); 7 % 2 = 1 \(remainder\); -7 // 2 = -4 because floor division rounds down toward minus infinity \(-3.5 becomes -4, not -3\).)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1568 0 R
/Next 1822 0 R
>>
endobj
1701 0 obj
<<
/Title (5. Comparison: integer paise \(store 33.40 as 3340\): exact and fast, but you must convert in and out and be careful with multiplication by rates. round\(\) on floats: hides the error when printing/rounding, but the stored numbers are still)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1568 0 R
/Prev 1823 0 R
/First 1824 0 R
/Last 1824 0 R
/Count -1
>>
endobj
1702 0 obj
<<
/Title (Result: 33400.29999999999857893118278 - wrong. float\('33.40'\) is already the inexact binary value 33.39999999999999857891..., and Decimal\(float\) copies that inexact value exactly. So the float error is imported into the Decimal. Decimal is only exact if it is created from a string \(Decimal\('33.40'\)\) or an integer. \(Decimal prints 28 significant digits by default.\))
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1570 0 R
>>
endobj
