1821 0 obj
<<
/Title (Integers: number of days \(10\), step counts \(fat_steps, snf_steps\), number of farmers \(300\). Floating-point numbers: litres, fat %, SNF %, rate, payment \(or Decimal for exact money\). Booleans: validity result of an entry \(True/False\), result of comparisons such as 2.0 <= fat <= 7.0. Strings: farmer ID, shift.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1698 0 R
>>
endobj
1822 0 obj
<<
/Title (3. 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 is False. Computers store floats in binary. 0.1 and 0.2 cannot be written exactly in binary \(like 1/3 in decimal\), so each is stored as the nearest binary fraction. Their sum is 0.30000000000000004, which is not the same float as 0.3. With Decimal\('0.1'\) + Decimal\('0.2'\) the digits are stored in decimal, so the result is exactly 0.3 and the comparison is True.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1568 0 R
/Prev 1700 0 R
/Next 1823 0 R
>>
endobj
1823 0 obj
<<
/Title (4. Why floats are risky for money: prices such as 33.40 are not exactly representable, and every addition can add a tiny error. Over 1,003 additions the float total was 33400.30000000061 instead of 33400.30. The error is small, but accounts must match to the paisa, equality checks fail, and errors can change rounded totals.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1568 0 R
/Prev 1822 0 R
/Next 1701 0 R
>>
endobj
