1832 0 obj
<<
/Title (How it works: the rates are kept in a file such as {'base': '30.00', 'fat_step': '0.40', 'snf_step': '0.30'}; at start-up the program reads the file with json.load\(\) into a dictionary and converts the strings to Decimal. Start-up time: O\(t\) for t rate values - measured 1.8 microseconds for 3 values, 0.2 ms for 1,000 and about 30 ms for 100,000. Space: O\(t\) \(the 100,000-value file was about 1.8 MB\). Maintenance: rates can be revised by editing the file with no change to Python code and no redistribution of the program after each rate revision; the cost is that the file can be missing or malformed, so the program must validate it when loading \(store numbers as strings or use parse_float=Decimal so no float errors are introduced\). For the few rate values here the load cost is negligible, so file-based rates are a good improvement for maintainability.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1706 0 R
>>
endobj
1833 0 obj
<<
/Title (2. What must be modified: Constants: one rate card is no longer enough - two cards \(normal and April-June\) plus BONUS_THRESHOLD_LITRES = 500 and BONUS_PER_LITRE = 0.50. Expressions: step counts can no longer be rounded to a whole step \(3.77% is 2.7 steps\). Functions: rate_per_litre and payout need the rate card as a parameter; a new select_rate_card\(month\) and bonus\(total_litres\) are needed; ten_day_total must add the bonus; validation must accept 2-decimal readings.)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1572 0 R
/Prev 1707 0 R
/Next 1708 0 R
>>
endobj
