1899 0 obj
<<
/Title (A farmer can only be paid a whole number of paise, so the bank really transfers the sum of the individually rounded payments. Rounding only the final total gives a different number \(about Rs 19.58 less for 150,000 payments in this experiment\), which would not match what was actually paid to the farmers. Therefore round every payment to 2 decimals \(preferably with Decimal\) and add the rounded values. \(The test payments here are synthetic, with 4 decimal places before rounding; the point - rounding position changes the total - holds for real data.\))
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1828 0 R
>>
endobj
1900 0 obj
<<
/Title (1. Data types and rounding: use Decimal created from strings for fat %, SNF %, rates and money \(or integer paise\), integers for step counts and days, str for IDs and shift. Round every payment to paise with ROUND_HALF_UP \(the usual commercial rule\), then add the rounded payments \(Task 10\). Keep the rate card as named data \(module constants / dictionary\) separate from the formulas.)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1831 0 R
/First 1919 0 R
/Last 1919 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 1920 0 R
>>
endobj
1901 0 obj
<<
